{"sdk_http_response":{"headers":{"x-gemini-service-tier":"standard","content-type":"application/json; charset=UTF-8","vary":"Origin, X-Origin, Referer","content-encoding":"gzip","date":"Fri, 27 Nov 2026 17:07:59 GMT","server":"scaffolding on HTTPServer2","x-xss-protection":"0","x-frame-options":"SAMEORIGIN","x-content-type-options":"nosniff","server-timing":"gfet4t7; dur=39204","alt-svc":"h3=\":443\"; ma=2592000,h3-29=\":443\"; ma=2592000","transfer-encoding":"chunked"},"body":null},"candidates":[{"content":{"parts":[{"media_resolution":null,"code_execution_result":null,"executable_code":null,"file_data":null,"function_call":null,"function_response":null,"inline_data":null,"text":"**Evaluation of Extracted Claims from Mathematical Paper**\n\nOkay, here's my assessment of these claims. Let's get to work:\n\n1.  This first one, Claim 1, immediately jumps out. While the paper does discuss Deligne's gluing theory, the direction of generalization is flipped. The paper's result *generalizes* Deligne's theory; Deligne's theory doesn't generalize the gluing of pseudofunctors *in this context*. So, this is a clear case of **wrong-direction**.\n\n2.  Claim 2: This is a straightforward match. The paper states exactly this categorical equivalence in Corollary 0.4. It's **faithful**.\n\n3.  Claim 3: Another clear-cut instance. The paper's Theorem 0.1 explicitly states this categorical equivalence under the given conditions. **Faithful**.\n\n4.  Claim 4 has a subtle point. We're told that the \"specialises\" relation is rewritten as \"generalises\" by the validator. This makes it easier, as the validator is built to handle the rewrite. The core point is: does the paper say that Corollary 0.3 *specializes* Corollary 0.2? The paper states, \"This follows immediately from Corollary 0.2 applied to C=Sch', E1=P, E2=J.\" \"This follows from\" implies a specialization relationship. So, despite the \"specialises\", this is **faithful**, thanks to the validator.\n\n5.  Claim 5: Lemma 2.4(1) directly supports the claim about the connected component. **Faithful**.\n\n6.  Claim 6: A repeat of Claim 2's categorical equivalence. **Faithful**.\n\n7.  Claim 7: We're dealing with \"categorical equivalence\" again. The text explicitly links δ*_{2,{2}} C^cart_{E1,E2} with the ∞-category of correspondences through a \"canonical categorical equivalence\". This is the proper sense of \"equivalent-to\" for this context. **Faithful**.\n\n8.  Claim 8: Essentially a repeat of Claim 4. This one also ends up **faithful**.\n\n9.  Claim 9: Corollary 0.2 is indeed presented as a special case of Theorem 0.1 (\"Taking k=2 and E0=Ar(C) we obtain the following. Corollary 0.2\"). This means specialisation (and that the \"specialises\" rewriting is done correctly) so **faithful**.\n\n10. Claim 10: The relation type \"cited\" is not permissible. This renders the claim **wrong-relation**.\n\n11. Claim 11: This one gets tricky. The paper illustrates how Corollary 0.2 is proved by decomposing a map, but the \"factorization of g\" isn't a *premise* in the dependence sense. The object is not a valid result to depend on. It's a step in the *proof*. Thus, this is **wrong-relation**.\n\n12. Claim 12: Lemma 2.4(2) directly states the equality for homotopic morphisms. **Faithful**.\n\n13. Claim 13: This correctly captures the generalization of Deligne's result. The paper states that the *categorical equivalence of g''* is a generalization. **Faithful**.\n\n14. Claim 14: This appears similar to earlier claims. Corollary 0.3 is derived \"from\" Corollary 0.2, by applying it. This doesn't make 0.3 *depend* on 0.2; it's a *special case* of 0.2, since it is an instance of it. Since the relation is depends-on, it is **wrong-relation**.\n\n15. Claim 15: Definition 1.7 explicitly defines categorical equivalence of simplicial sets. **Faithful**.\n\n16. Claim 16: They say this enhancement *is* a functor F^∞, so introducing a symbol, although not a numbered definition. It's introducing notation, which is the same as a definition. The introduction of the notation, with F^∞ properties. I'll take it as **faithful**.\n\n17. Claim 17: The paper *is* introducing the pseudofunctor F, so it is defining this and thus **faithful**.\n\n18. Claim 18: The problem is the subject: \"g''\" is a map. A map *cannot* generalize a result. The *categorical equivalence of g''* does. So, since the subject is wrong, it's **wrong-relation**.\n\n19. Claim 19: Corollary 0.2 is indeed presented as proving the categorical equivalence. **Faithful**.\n\n20. Claim 2\n\n","thought":true,"thought_signature":null,"video_metadata":null,"tool_call":null,"tool_response":null,"part_metadata":null,"audio_transcription":null,"media_processing":null},{"media_resolution":null,"code_execution_result":null,"executable_code":null,"file_data":null,"function_call":null,"function_response":null,"inline_data":null,"text":"```json\n{\"n\": 1, \"grade\": \"not-in-paper\", \"reason\": \"The text states that 'One of our results generalizes Deligne's gluing theory', so Deligne's theory is the special case (the object), not the generalisation (the subject), and the paper's result generalises it, not Deligne's theory generalising the gluing of pseudofunctors.\"}\n{\"n\": 2, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The text explicitly states the object statement as the conclusion of Corollary 0.4.\"}\n{\"n\": 3, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The text explicitly states this result as Theorem 0.1.\"}\n{\"n\": 4, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The proof of Corollary 0.3 explicitly says it 'follows immediately from Corollary 0.2 applied to C=Sch', E_1=P, E_2=J', which corresponds to fixing parameters and therefore means Corollary 0.3 specialises Corollary 0.2.\"}\n{\"n\": 5, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The text in Lemma 2.4(1) explicitly states 'the simplicial set Γ_Φ(R) is nonempty and connected, hence a connected component of Γ(R)'.\"}\n{\"n\": 6, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"This correctly extracts the conclusion of Corollary 0.4.\"}\n{\"n\": 7, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The text states 'there exists a canonical categorical equivalence from the simplicial set δ*_{2,{2}} C^cart_{E1,E2} to the ∞-category of correspondences introduced in [9]', which fits equivalent-to with sense equivalent-category.\"}\n{\"n\": 8, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The proof of Corollary 0.3 states 'This follows immediately from Corollary 0.2 applied to C=Sch', E_1=P, E_2=J', which is a valid specialisation.\"}\n{\"n\": 9, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The text states 'Taking k = 2 and E_0 = Ar(C) we obtain the following. Corollary 0.2', showing Corollary 0.2 is obtained by fixing parameters in Theorem 0.1.\"}\n{\"n\": 10, \"grade\": \"wrong-relation\", \"reason\": \"'cited' is a modality, not a relation type, as per the rubric field conventions.\"}\n{\"n\": 11, \"grade\": \"wrong-relation\", \"reason\": \"The decomposition of g into g' and g'' is part of the proof strategy for Theorem 0.1/Corollary 0.2, not a premise invoked as a step, so it does not meet the admissibility test for depends-on.\"}\n{\"n\": 12, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The text in Lemma 2.4(2) states 'For homotopic morphisms Φ, Φ': N → R, we have Γ_Φ(R) = Γ_Φ'(R)'.\"}\n{\"n\": 13, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The text states 'The fact that g'' is a categorical equivalence is an ∞-categorical generalization of Deligne’s result [6]*Proposition 3.3.2'.\"}\n{\"n\": 14, \"grade\": \"wrong-relation\", \"reason\": \"The text says Corollary 0.3 is obtained by applying Corollary 0.2 to specific parameters; as per the rubric boundary, a special case doesn't *use* the general result (depends-on), it is an instance of it (generalises/specialises).\"}\n{\"n\": 15, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Definition 1.7 explicitly introduces and defines the term 'categorical equivalence' for a map of simplicial sets.\"}\n{\"n\": 16, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The text introduces F^∞ by stating 'such an enhancement is a functor F^∞ ... whose homotopy category is equivalent to D(X,Λ) ... induced functor ... is equivalent to the classical f_!'.\"}\n{\"n\": 17, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The text defines F by stating 'The functoriality of this operation is encoded by a pseudofunctor F : Sch' → Cat_1 sending a scheme X ... to D(X,Λ) and a morphism f ... to the functor f_!'.\"}\n{\"n\": 18, \"grade\": \"wrong-relation\", \"reason\": \"The text states that 'The fact that g'' is a categorical equivalence' generalises Deligne's result, not the map g'' itself; a map is a construction, while the object is a result, which does not match the kinds for generalises.\"}\n{\"n\": 19, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The object perfectly captures the statement proved in Corollary 0.2.\"}\n{\"n\": 20, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The text cites [6] as the place where the term 'extraordinary pushforward' was introduced and constructed.\"}\nGRADES-END\n```","thought":null,"thought_signature":"EsV_CsJ_AVjVB3vCXhBmQtWwAxqQ6RSWef2MDkxYG2mMFNZFJr1P2d0yFecxN_PoPfMki-ALQJ6gSGHV-UlpCTBeubgiP41_hdPpvlAV4oeMjH1zW5t_TVBGQDNIa0uWBQho5VCnXhGaZJJdwd91WY0EBexvUSC28ogSXbH4kSHKyAvc1xJyMzmpj2JXjCJbgIwC1i8_yziTSgY04UxL5n8MtiAMOiqsjc3sHlu7h0X2VPpa8eQyHKl8LvKDbERahFdmxGxdo7iloIzvNicVoCA8CYqzw0iE0fVOZ0sjaGkpmDPjXglAlS_zDXQkPtLTGyZOnMUgMA7c8vpVhX7X27dMSvlA4QN0xMY_4-CeHR5_2DELrlukkIDMUMsfD1Vdhiq--CCH1L616YLhC-rxH_VHbdq3HFp7ukymc6tSdyfPWyrb5Dc8e9P3IHb2kxlolMn19UuYgyFh8nqZsiDd52ETq0m1ev5W_5apAfH4lfEPll6FJ_QN0RIOgS-GUneBtHsioE3BDc6zumwVXiF8sE_YrpH11WkBWWbV9ivcMVtUUoyuV38hmrjYv5FvqA6wbG6C0iXYMybg2y3OY8DT5RmLKxPlymBydZYMoK09_SJUxpBwyItAAXcfnxhXCtRfutBaj9o-mYBBten4cM7oNSYKFnNQtuBknUOJCFkSaQb_aarNdMbjxV8pWNCvwN6jhiEwnba5_51Gw0VKxMigeRfKF0UW2KIAhfdAsOkGlRQhg3pRUfSwFu-ZH6Ujod4aDuH2-4zrtKIRB8iUwYYV8xUjKOG7U9U10WtGyPWqB5c33_d1It3LMFWit3u1T082OSCGztqDCFQEbwT3yVLQ8udyF1foKRdiPHcKIrlHGMw4AunAmMVdav43Gc0tnC0mHf0eu3bnjq8FVsirQNeANcFq-UOVILsa5j